// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/OWNERSHIP.md R2.a (a move inside a loop is a use-after-move
// on the next iteration).
//
// This is the file that pays for loops. docs/OWNERSHIP.md section 0.3 chose
// lexical loans on the stated ground that they need no control-flow graph and
// can be decided in one pass with a scope stack. A loop is a back edge, and it
// breaks that assumption directly.
//
// `take(owned buf)` is fine the first time through. On the second iteration
// `buf` has already been moved out of, so the same line is a use-after-move.
// The single-pass checker marks `buf` dead once and never revisits it, so
// without R2.a nothing catches it.
//
// Measured:
//   error: 'buf' is moved inside a loop, so the next iteration would use it
//          after the move
//   note: 'buf' is declared outside this loop; assign to it before the end of
//         the body to revive it
//
// The rule is deliberately CONSERVATIVE and the note says how to satisfy it:
// reassigning `buf` before the body ends revives it (R3a) and the loop is
// accepted. A body that always `break`s before reaching the move is still
// rejected, because this rule does not track which paths reach the end. That
// is the same trade section 0.3 already made, with the same guarantee:
// everything accepted now stays accepted under a real control-flow analysis,
// so tightening now and relaxing later never breaks source compatibility.

pub struct Buffer { copy len: Int }

pub fn take(owned b: Buffer) { }

pub fn main() {
    var owned buf = Buffer { len: 0 }
    var i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        take(owned buf)
        i = i + 1
    }
}
